WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, I'm
Martin Omander, and today

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.330
I'll tell you about a pain point
from my previous developer jobs

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:09.240
and how to solve it
with modern technology.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.276
I wished I'd had this
technology back then.

00:00:12.276 --> 00:00:15.192
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:24.920
Before my current job as a
Developer Advocate at Google,

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:28.130
I worked at a string of
startups as a software engineer.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.290
I always struggled
with how to store

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:32.689
passwords, API
keys, certificates,

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:34.520
and other sensitive data.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.760
If I hardcoded these secrets
into the source code,

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.620
I had two problems.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.530
One, anyone with access
to the source repo

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.150
could see the secrets.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.160
They could copy the
secret somewhere else,

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.340
and we didn't have auditing
and access controls.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:54.230
And two, it would be hard to
move code between dev, test,

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:55.670
and production environments.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:59.190
Secrets typically differ
between environments.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:02.720
So in my previous job, I did
what the Twelve-Factor App

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.400
methodology recommends.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.670
I put the secrets in
environment variables.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.460
That way they were not
part of the code base.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:13.010
But the secrets had
to be stored somewhere

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:15.560
so I put them in a file,
which I had to be careful

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.960
not to check into
source control.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.080
At one job, we burned
secrets to a CD

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.200
that we kept in the
CIO's desk drawer.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.540
Handling files without
source control is awkward.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.120
I remember thinking
that there must

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.980
be a better way of doing it.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.380
Secret Manager and
Google Cloud platform

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.210
provides that better way.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:38.570
It has been generally
available since March 2020.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:40.910
We recently added
native integration

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:44.060
with Cloud Run, which makes
it easy to mount secrets

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.680
in Cloud Run services.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.960
Let's see how we can update
an existing Cloud Run

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.630
service to use Secret Manager.

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:52.970
The service is a
simple API that returns

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:57.530
information about different
dog breeds, like this.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:00.620
The code reads database,
user, password, et cetera,

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.260
from environment variables
when it creates the database

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.690
connection pool here.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.210
The environment
variables are shown here

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:10.729
in the Cloud Console
in plaintext.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:17.370
So let's say we want a
better control and auditing

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:22.500
of the database password by
moving it into Secret Manger.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.520
We see here that the Cloud
Run servers runs as Dog Breed

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.610
API, a custom service account.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:31.740
I used the principle
of least privilege

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.200
when I created this
account, so it only

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.710
has the Cloud SQL client role.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.290
This means that it can
connect to Cloud SQL

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.050
databases but nothing else.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:46.270
We will need to remember this
account for the next step.

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:49.510
Next, I will go to the
Secret Manager in my project,

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:51.840
create a new secret for
the database password,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.120
and enter its value here.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.540
Now, let's see who can
access this password.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.510
The Dog Breed API is
not in the list yet.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:01.890
Let's add it.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:06.360
Again, we're applying the
principle of least privilege.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.640
The Dog Breed API
account currently

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.930
has access to db_password, but
not any of the other secrets

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.220
that we see here.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.500
This reduces risk.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.860
Now, I'm ready to go back
to the Cloud Run service.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:23.910
First, I remove the DB_PASS
plaintext environment variable.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.310
I feel better already.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:29.880
Then, I'll add it
back, but instead

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:31.920
of entering the
actual password, I

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.290
will point DB_PASS to the
password in Secret Manager.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:41.910
Then I click DEPLOY here so
this change takes effect.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.430
And look, the API still works.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:48.390
So, what did we achieve here?

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:52.350
Well first, we can now use the
principle of least privilege

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.570
for the password
and other secrets.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.610
We can define
granular permissions

00:03:56.610 --> 00:04:01.170
for who can access which secrets
and who can add new secrets.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:02.970
For example, we
just configured it

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:06.330
so our Cloud Run service
can access, but not write,

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.940
db_password in production.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.220
And if only a few
people in our dev team

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.620
are allowed to handle
secrets in production,

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:17.740
we reduce the risk of downtime
due to silly mistakes.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:18.490
I know.

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:21.329
I once mistakenly deleted
the production database

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:22.650
at a previous job.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:26.610
Second, we now have
auditing of this password.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.940
By default, whenever it
is created or updated

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.610
we'll get a log entry like this.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.497
I usually also turn
on additional logging

00:04:35.497 --> 00:04:37.080
so there will be an
entry for whenever

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.060
a secret is accessed as well.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.600
And we achieved all
this without changing

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:44.790
a single line of source code.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:47.460
Delightful.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.860
But what if our existing
code reads secrets

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:53.190
from a file instead of
environment variables?

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:54.360
No problem.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.200
We can configure Cloud Run so
that secrets look like files

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.430
to the source code.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.260
Pretty neat.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:04.410
I should also mention there is
a third way of accessing secrets

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:05.910
from source code.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:09.210
Our code could make a call
to the Secret Manager client

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:12.240
library every time
it needs a secret.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.750
This obviously means that we
have to update our source code,

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.480
but every time we call
the client library

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.720
we will get the current
value of the secret.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.740
So this method is useful if we
expect secrets to change often

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:28.530
and we don't want to redeploy
our Cloud Run servers

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:30.610
every time that happens.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:33.690
The same is true when we
read secrets as a file.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:36.390
If we access secrets as
environment variables

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:39.630
and we update a secret, we
need to redeploy the Cloud Run

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.090
service to get the latest
value of that secret.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:43.800
This has been an
introduction to using

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.350
Cloud Run with Secret Manager.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:48.390
The docs have a lot
more information,

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:51.330
like best practices for
how to manage permissions,

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.910
how to rotate your
secrets to reduce risk,

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:56.700
and how to version secrets.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.500
Also, in the
description below, you

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:01.620
will find a link to
the source code I used.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:03.360
I really hope that
this makes it easier

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:06.180
for you to build secure systems.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.090
As developers, our
code may be entrusted

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:11.700
with some very sensitive data.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.430
We carry a big responsibility.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.050
Let's live up to it.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.360
Please let me know if
this video was helpful

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.100
or if you have any questions.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.630
Also, if you have
suggestions for new episodes,

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:24.460
please add them in
the comments below.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.660
Until next time.

